10:32:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: No. Summer is mainly hotter because Earth’s axis is tilted about 23.5°. When your hemisphere is tilted toward the Sun: - The Sun appears higher in the sky, so its light is more concentrated on the ground. - Days are longer, giving the surface more time to warm. - Sunlight travels through less atmosphere, so more energy reaches the ground. Earth’s distance from the Sun changes only a little over the year. In fact, Earth is closest to the Sun in early January, during Northern Hemisphere winter, and farthest away in early July, during Northern Hemisphere summer. The seasons are opposite in the two hemispheres because when one hemisphere is tilted toward the Sun, the other is tilted away.
