10:32:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Being closer to a heat source does make a difference—your bonfire analogy is correct in general. But for Earth’s seasons, the distance change is too small and happens at the “wrong” time to explain Northern Hemisphere summer. Earth is: - **Closest to the Sun in early January** (about 147 million km away) - **Farthest in early July** (about 152 million km away) So during Northern Hemisphere summer, Earth is actually slightly *farther* from the Sun. That distance change does affect the amount of sunlight Earth receives—Earth gets roughly **7% more solar energy** in January than in July—but the effect of the tilt is much larger for any given hemisphere. In Northern summer, the Northern Hemisphere is tilted toward the Sun, giving it longer days and more direct, concentrated sunlight. Meanwhile, the Southern Hemisphere has winter despite Earth being relatively close to the Sun. So: **distance matters a little, but Earth’s tilt is what causes the seasons.**
