10:33:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s understandable to think that—many school diagrams are misleading because they draw Earth’s orbit as a very stretched oval. In reality, Earth’s orbit is almost circular, so the drawing exaggerates the distance difference. The key detail those diagrams should also show is Earth’s **tilted axis**: - In **Northern Hemisphere summer** (June–August), the North Pole tilts toward the Sun. The Sun is higher overhead and days are longer. - At that time, Earth is actually near its **farthest** point from the Sun. - In **Southern Hemisphere summer** (December–February), Earth is closer to the Sun—but it is summer there because the Southern Hemisphere is tilted toward the Sun. A simple reality check is that Australia and the United States have opposite seasons at the same time. Earth cannot be “closer to the Sun” for one hemisphere and “farther” for the other; the difference is which hemisphere is tilted toward the Sun. So the orbit diagrams were likely meant to show Earth moving around the Sun, but if they implied that proximity causes summer, they were oversimplified or incorrect.
