10:33:37 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=1066
10:33:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The important issue is not whether distance matters—it does—but **how large the distance change is compared with the angle change**. A heater six inches closer can matter a lot if you were only a few feet away. But Earth is about **150 million km** from the Sun, and its distance changes by only about **5 million km** either side of that: roughly **3% closer or farther**. That produces about a **7% change** in incoming solar energy. By contrast, the change in the Sun’s angle can easily change heating by a factor of several. For example, sunlight hitting the ground when the Sun is high—say 70° above the horizon—is concentrated into a relatively small patch. At 20° above the horizon, the same beam is spread over roughly **three times as much ground**, so each square metre gets only about one-third as much direct energy. It also travels through much more atmosphere at the low angle. And summer adds much longer daylight. At many mid-latitude locations, a summer day might have 16 hours of daylight versus 8 hours in winter. So there is both: 1. **More intense sunlight per square metre**, because of the angle, and 2. **More hours of sunlight**, because of the tilt. That overwhelms the modest Earth–Sun distance effect. The decisive evidence is that in early January Earth is closest to the Sun, yet it is winter in North America and Europe and summer in Australia and southern South America. One Earth–Sun distance cannot produce opposite seasons simultaneously; the hemispheres’ opposite tilts do.
