10:33:51 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=1063 miss=374
10:33:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It may not have been *completely* wrong, but it was likely oversimplified or talking about a different timescale. For Earth’s ordinary year-to-year seasons, the primary cause is unequivocally **axial tilt**, not orbital distance. The direct test is that the two hemispheres have opposite seasons at the same moment. Earth’s elliptical orbit does have effects: - It makes Earth receive about **7% more solar energy in early January** than in early July. - It slightly modifies the strength and length of seasons. Because Earth moves faster when nearer the Sun, Northern Hemisphere winter is currently a little shorter than its summer. - Over tens of thousands of years, changes in Earth’s orbit and the direction of its axis—part of the **Milankovitch cycles**—help alter climate and contribute to ice-age cycles. But it cannot be the basic explanation for summer versus winter. Earth is closest to the Sun around **January 3**, when it is winter in the Northern Hemisphere and summer in the Southern Hemisphere. The tilt explains both at once: whichever hemisphere is tilted toward the Sun gets higher, more direct sunlight and longer days. So if the documentary said changing Earth–Sun distance is *the* cause of the annual seasons, that portion was inaccurate. If it said orbital shape can *influence* seasonal intensity or long-term climate, that is correct.
