10:34:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s a very common belief, partly because it matches everyday intuition and because many orbit illustrations are misleading. Lots of people can share the same misconception—especially when it is reinforced by simplified explanations. But this is easy to check with observations: - **Northern and Southern Hemispheres have opposite seasons at the same time.** July is summer in Canada but winter in Argentina. Earth cannot be simultaneously closer to the Sun for one hemisphere and farther for the other. - **Earth is closest to the Sun in early January.** That is winter in the Northern Hemisphere. - **The Sun’s daily path changes strongly through the year.** In Northern summer it rises farther north, reaches a much higher noon position, and sets later. Those longer, more direct days deliver substantially more heat. Distance does have a small influence: because Earth is closer to the Sun during Southern Hemisphere summer, Southern summers are, all else equal, slightly more intense. Oceans and land distribution complicate the real-world temperatures, but the effect exists. So your friends and family are not unreasonable for thinking it; it is just a widely repeated, intuitive explanation that does not fit the global evidence.
