10:34:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Distance absolutely affects temperature. Sunlight follows the inverse-square law: if Earth were significantly closer to the Sun, it would receive more energy and become warmer; farther away, less energy and cooler. The point is that Earth’s yearly distance change is modest: - Earth–Sun distance varies by about **3%** above or below the average. - That causes about a **7% difference** in incoming sunlight between closest and farthest points. - Earth is closest in **early January** and farthest in **early July**. So distance is a real, measurable secondary effect. It slightly strengthens Southern Hemisphere summer and Northern Hemisphere winter under today’s orbit. But axial tilt changes, for each hemisphere, both the **concentration of sunlight** on the ground and the **number of daylight hours**—often by much more than 7%. That is why tilt determines which hemisphere has summer, while distance modestly adjusts how strong the seasons are. So the accurate answer is neither “distance causes seasons” nor “distance makes zero difference”: **distance matters, but Earth’s tilt is the dominant cause of annual seasons.**
