10:34:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes—the same distance rule applies throughout the Solar System. Mercury receives far more sunlight than Neptune because it is vastly closer to the Sun. The difference is **scale**: - Mercury is about **58 million km** from the Sun. - Neptune is about **4.5 billion km** away. - Earth’s distance over one orbit changes only from about **147 million km** to **152 million km**. Because sunlight falls off with the square of distance, Mercury and Neptune receive enormously different amounts of solar energy. Earth’s January-to-July change is comparatively small: about **7%** in sunlight received. That 7% is real. If all else were equal, it makes Earth slightly warmer near closest approach and cooler near farthest approach. But it affects the whole planet in the same direction at once. It cannot create Northern Hemisphere summer and Southern Hemisphere winter simultaneously. Earth’s tilt, meanwhile, changes: - which hemisphere gets the Sun high in the sky, - how concentrated the sunlight is per square metre, - and how many hours of daylight occur. So Mercury-versus-Neptune supports the claim that distance matters. It does not show that Earth’s small annual distance variation is the main cause of Earth’s seasons.
