10:35:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It does make natural sense, and it is basic physics: **closer to the Sun means more energy per square metre.** Nothing about the tilt explanation denies that. The only reason angle enters is that the question is not “what affects heating?” but “why does one hemisphere have summer while the other has winter?” Distance is shared by the entire Earth at any moment. Tilt makes the two hemispheres receive sunlight differently. Think of standing near a bonfire while holding your hands: - Move closer: both hands get more heat. - Keep the same distance but turn one hand toward the fire and the other edge-on: one hand warms much more. Earth’s orbit-distance change is like moving the whole body a little closer or farther: it affects everyone. Earth’s tilt is like orienting the Northern and Southern Hemispheres differently: one receives more direct, longer-lasting sunlight while the other receives less. So your intuition is right about distance. It just answers a different part of the problem than the one that distinguishes summer from winter.
