10:35:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s understandable to feel shaken by that. But finding one mistaken or oversimplified idea doesn’t mean everything you learned is unreliable—or that you were careless for believing it. School often teaches useful first-pass models. Sometimes diagrams or wording leave out key conditions, and common explanations get repeated even when they are incomplete. The good part of science is that claims can be checked against observations rather than accepted purely on authority. For this one, you can verify the key facts independently: - Look up the dates of **perihelion** (closest approach, around early January) and **aphelion** (farthest, around early July). - Compare season calendars for places in the Northern and Southern Hemispheres. - Observe the Sun’s path: in local summer, it is higher at noon and daylight lasts longer. That doesn’t mean you need to distrust everything. A healthier approach is: treat what you learned as a starting point, ask what evidence supports it, and update when better detail is available. That’s not losing trust in learning—it’s using the skill learning is supposed to build.
