10:37:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not really—“more direct” and “closer” are different geometrical effects. When the Sun is higher in the sky, you are only very slightly closer to it in straight-line distance: at most about one Earth radius, roughly **6,400 km**, compared with an Earth–Sun distance of about **150 million km**. That is a change of only about **0.004%**, far too small to cause seasonal heating. The important change is how the same incoming sunlight is spread across the ground: - With the Sun high overhead, a beam illuminates a relatively small area, so each square metre gets more energy. - With the Sun low in the sky, that beam is stretched over a much larger area, so each square metre gets less energy. - Low-angle sunlight also travels through more atmosphere before reaching the ground. So it is fair in an everyday sense to say the Sun feels “more intense” in summer, but physically that intensity comes mainly from **the angle at which light hits the surface**, not meaningful closeness to the Sun.
