10:38:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I understand why proximity feels like the natural explanation, and it is true that it affects how much sunlight Earth receives. But for Earth’s yearly seasons, it cannot be the primary driver. The simplest decisive observation is: - In **early January**, Earth is **closest** to the Sun. - It is then **winter in the Northern Hemisphere** and **summer in the Southern Hemisphere**. The Earth–Sun distance is essentially the same for everyone on Earth at that moment. A shared distance effect can make the whole planet receive a bit more solar energy, but it cannot make Canada cold and Australia warm simultaneously. “Leaning toward the Sun” is not dressing it up: it changes the Sun’s height in the sky and the length of the day, which changes the solar energy received per square metre. That is why it drives the seasonal contrast between hemispheres. So the accurate combined view is: - **Distance:** affects the planet’s total solar input somewhat. - **Axial tilt:** causes the Northern-versus-Southern summer/winter cycle.
